## Section 2: Testing Theory

The Pex method, as I've understood it, employs the principle of equivalence classes. An equivalence class is a set of values in an ordered set such that a particular value can be replaced with any value from the set without changing the output of a function. Therefore, all inputs corresponding to a single equivalence class should have the same behavior.

In this case, inputs should be taken from the ArrayList class, which is a part of the system under test. The testing process should be carried out by creating several tests, each checking that the 'Contains' method works as expected with different types of input data, including different equivalence classes.

I will create several different test cases, which will simulate different circumstances that could occur. The tests will cover the following cases:

### 1. Test Case 1 - Null input
In this case, the input is `null`, so it represents an exceptional condition. Let's check how the 'Contains' method behaves.

### 2. Test Case 2 - Empty ArrayList
The input is an empty `ArrayList`, which is another exceptional condition. The results are expected to be different from the previous one.

### 3. Test Case 3 - ArrayList with one item
An `ArrayList` containing just one item. The behavior of the 'Contains' method should be the same as a case with a single equivalent class.

### 4. Test Case 4 - ArrayList with more than one item; x is included
`x` is included in the `ArrayList`. The 'Contains' method is expected to return `true`.

### 5. Test Case 5 - ArrayList with more than one item; x is not included
As the name suggests, `x` is not included in the `ArrayList`. The 'Contains' method is expected to return `false`.

### 6. Test Case 6 - ArrayList with one equivalent class
The ArrayList contains only equivalent items i.e. all items are equivalent to each other according to the specified equivalence relation. The 'Contains' method should return `true` both times.

### 7. Test Case 7 - ArrayList with multiple equivalent classes
The ArrayList contains items from different equivalence classes. The 'Contains' method should return `true` when the item is included in a class that `x` belongs to and `false` otherwise.

## Section 3: Testing Plan

Since the ArrayList class is quite large and complex, comprehensive test coverage may not be possible due to the large number and variety of different use cases. The key to our coverage of the ArrayList class would be to thoroughly test the 'Contains' method and closely follow the principles of equivalence classes. Specifically, we plan to:

- Test for corner cases in the ArrayList class (null, empty list, single-item list).
- Test for--------------------            Assert.IsFalse(New_Ret?"False" : "True");

