### [36. ArrayList Methods Test](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-basics?view=vs-2019)

The process of testing all methods of a class individually is known as “[Unit Testing](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-basics?view=vs-2019).” These tests are performed with a framework such as NUnit or MSTest.

One of the main benefits of unit tests is that they help ensure that each component of your application works as intended. By isolating these units and testing them individually, you can increase your confidence in the overall stability and functionality of your application.

In most cases, unit tests are performed using Test-Driven Development, in which tests are written before the code to be tested. This ensures that the correct functionality is developed. But, if the code to be tested already exists, tests can still be written to ensure that it behaves as expected. This is often referred to as “[Test-First Development](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-basics?view=vs-2019).”

NUnit is a free open-source unit-testing framework for all CLR languages. It is actively maintained by the NUnit Software Foundation and is used in numerous commercial and open source projects.

Here's an example of how you might test the Add method of an ArrayList.

```java
[TestFixture]
public class ArrayListTest
{
    private ArrayList list;

    // Setup method that runs before every test
    [SetUp]
    public void Initialize()
    {
        list = new ArrayList();
    }

    [Test]
    public void Add_WhenCalledWithValidArgument_ShouldAddElementToTheList()
    {
        list.Add(1);
 
        Assert.That(list, Has.Member(1));
    }

    [Test]
    public void Add_WhenCalledWithInvalidArgument_ShouldThrowAnException()
    {
        Assert.Throws<ArgumentNullException>(() => list.Add(null));
    }
}
```

The `[TestFixture]` attribute represents a set of tests that should be run together. The `[SetUp]` method is called before every test in the fixture is run.

The first test, `Add_WhenCalledWithValidArgument_ShouldAddElementToTheList`, simply calls the `Add` method with an argument and checks if the element is in the list by using NUnit‘s `Has.Member` constraint.

The second test, `Add_WhenCalledWithInvalidArgument_ShouldThrowAnException`, checks whether calling `Add` with `null` throws an `ArgumentNullException`.

