--------------------            if (New_Ret == false)
            {
                if (Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != -1)
                {
                    Assert.Fail();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The test case contributes to the property `arrMap.Count = -1` iff `New_Ret = false`.

### Invariant:

No element is added in the `arrList`, `arrList2`, and all elements in the `arrList3`.

### Framework assertion:

ArrayList.Test.ArrayListContractTest.PUT_ContainsContract

### Precondition

`arrList is a non-empty ArrayList, where all elements are unique`

### Postcondition

`arrList contains not any element`

## Solution

The problem is with the contract of the ArrayList. The PUT_ContainsContract method checks whether `arrList.Contains(x)` returns false if the list has no elements. However, after the invocation of `arrList.Contains(x)` all elements are removed, which means that there's absolutely no element which could return true to `arrList.Contains(x)`. The method expects that this condition holds after the execution of the tests but it's not guaranteed by the specification in our test case. The problem may occur due to the lack of any sort of synchronization between the test and the actual scenario. This means that if an element gets added back in the middle of a test, the invariant is no longer guaranteed, and the test may incorrectly fail. This leads to a situation similar to a race condition where an element gets added between the time you check and the time you observe the results of the check but before the check occurs again.

## Assertion in code

```C#
if (New_Ret == false)
{
    if (Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != -1)
    {
        Assert.Fail();
    }
}      
```
A failure of this assertion would indicate that the list has some elements which would make `Contains(x)` return true, violating the postcondition of the test.

## Conclusion

This issue demonstrates the importance of maintaining proper synchronization between the actual program's execution and rigorous testing. In the case of the `ContainsContract`, it's essential to confirm that the list remains unchanged during the process of checking the conditions in the test case. If the preconditions and postconditions of the contract are verified by these test cases, it guarantees the reliability and correctness of the ArrayList's design.

In practical test scenario, it's also recommended to use `[SetUp]` or `[TearDown]` attribute in the testing framework like nunit to manage the state (adding/removing elements in the ArrayList) between each test case. This is an important consideration when designing and writing tests for invariants involving multiple method calls.

## References

- https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/pfxteam/2012/05/05/coding-guidance-testing-code-that-throws-exceptions/
- https://stackoverflow.com/questions/3529549/c-sharp-nunit-testing-state-maintenance-between-tests
- https://github.com/Microsoft/vstest/blob/main/docs/states.md
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/how-to-use-test-settings-for-traits-to-filter-test-cases?view=vs-2019
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-microsoft-visualstudio-testtools-unittesting-members-in-unit-tests?view=vs-2019
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-microsoft-visualstudio-testtools-unittesting-members-in-unit-tests?view=vs-2019
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/getting-started-with-unit-testing-in-visual-studio?view=vs-2019
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/use-code-coverage-to-determine-how-much---------------------            Debug.Assert(New_Ret || Old_arrListCount != -1);
	// if return value is true, then old index of x should be equal or less than the new index of x
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret && Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX);
	/// if return value is true, then old last index of x should be equal or less than the new last index of x
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}
```

In the test, the method Contains of ArrayList is called. The observed values include the counts of the ArrayList before and after the operation, the indexes of the old and new array (Both IndexOf and LastIndexOf are called). The method Contains returns a boolean value and Assert statements ensure that if the return value is false, the old list's count is not -1. If the return value is true, it confirms that the old index of x is less than or equal to the new index of x, and the old last index of x is less than or equal to the new last index of x.

These assertions cover multiple aspects such as behavioral contracts, behavioral consistency, and the preservation of class invariants, which are generally accepted in software testing.

The PexObserve is there to allow the test to observe the values of variables in the test context. It helps to identify any unexpected variables in the test that may affect the behavior, but in most cases, it won't be very useful without a specific domain knowledge. 

For the particular case of Contains, the PexObserve would look insignificant because the Contains method itself does not manipulate variables or alter any other instance or class state in the ArrayList class in any way. PexObserve only helps in the test when we are sure that some other method call is going to change the state that we want to test.

Note: This is a test case to perform code coverage analysis and it does not really validate functionality (e.g., ArrayList's Contains method doesn't actually change the contents of the ArrayList, so this test doesn't validate whether it actually accomplishes its intended function). It rather verifies that the Contains method, as invoked in the test, doesn't incorrectly report elements that don't exist in the list, and correctly reports elements that do exist in the list.

So the important points to remember are when to use PexObserve, Understand PexStatic and PexAssembly, and how to use PexAssume and PexAssert to create effective tests.

Also one thing you can do when using PexObserve is to include it in a test method that is not directly involved in verification but you want to see the state or variables of the system during testing. For example, when constructing our test. We can use it after some state in the system has changed and before a different state occurs. Just to observe and understand how system state changes over time in our test scenario. But remember that in testing, the key objective is not just to observe system behavior, but also to assert that it behaves as expected in all of its different states. 

Source: https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/pexteam/2012/04/27/pexobserve-and-pexassembly/

In summary, PexObserve is primarily used for debugging and validating test cases to understand what's happening in the system under test. Assertions using PexAssert should be used to validate the behavior of your logic and not just testing methods invocation. PexObserve helps test developers understand how the system state changes and where it deviates from the expected behavior.

